            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA/JPL announced on **Aug. 5, 2026** that Perseverance captured a first-of-its-kind view from the surface of another planet: **Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos**. The image sequence was taken by the rover’s **Mastcam-Z** camera on **July 2, 2026**, during sol 1,907 of the mission, from the rim of Jezero Crater. NASA described it as a “unique Earth self-portrait” with a “Phobos photobomb”【5:0†source】.\n\nOther recent highlights:\n\n- **Perseverance has been studying “Broom Point,”** a layered bedrock unit on the rim of Jezero Crater that NASA says may be more than **3.9 billion years old**. NASA reported in July that the rover found evidence the 245-foot-thick rock stack was built by **repeated asteroid impacts**【5:3†source】.\n- The rover recently hit a major driving milestone: it completed the equivalent of a **marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km** — reaching that distance on its **1,890th Martian day**. That was far faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【5:4†source】.\n- Earlier in 2026, Perseverance also completed the **first AI-planned drives on another world**, using vision-capable generative AI to help create safe waypoints for navigating Jezero Crater’s rim【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, still driving beyond Jezero Crater, and the newest headline is its historic capture of **Earth being occulted by Phobos** from the Martian surface.",
